Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex regex(R"(\*(.*?)\*)");
    std::smatch match;
    while (std::regex_search(html, match, regex)) {
        html = html.replace(match.position(), match.length(), "<em>" + match.str(1) + "</em>");
    }
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a *markdown* text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match the asterisk (`*`) and the text within the asterisks. It then replaces the matched text with an HTML `<em>` tag to render it in emphasis. The `main()` function demonstrates how to use this function by converting a markdown string to HTML and printing both.

